# Nook settings for Docker (the "1-click" way: start.cmd / start.sh / docker compose up).
#
# Copy this file to ".env" IN THIS SAME FOLDER (next to docker-compose.yml). That is the only
# settings file Docker reads: it passes the settings on to the API, the worker and the website.
# (api/.env.example and web/.env.example are only for developers running `npm run dev`.)
#
# Lines starting with # are switched off; remove the # to switch one on.

# ─── What to start ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# app = the whole Nook. Add ",ai" for the AI Companion and/or ",monitoring" for Grafana,
# e.g. COMPOSE_PROFILES=app,ai   Then run `docker compose up -d` again.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=app

# ─── Basics ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Database password. Set it ONCE, before the very first start: the database keeps the first one.
# (Stage 20: start.cmd / start.sh replace "nook" with a random password on a brand-new install.)
# Changed it later and the API now says "password authentication failed"? Put the old one back,
# or start over with `docker compose down -v` (⚠️ deletes all Nook data).
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=nook

# Ports on this PC. If port 80 is already taken (Skype, IIS...), use 8080 and
# open http://<this PC's IP>:8080 instead.
HTTP_PORT=80
HTTPS_PORT=443

# Who can open Nook directly (Stage 20):
#   0.0.0.0    this PC and every device on your home network (default)
#   127.0.0.1  this PC only. Use it with Tailscale (`tailscale serve`) if only your own devices should get in,
#              even on your home Wi-Fi. Changed it? Run: docker compose up -d
BIND_IP=0.0.0.0

# How long Nook keeps data, in days (Stage 11).
# People who haven't opened Nook for DATA_RETENTION_DAYS are deleted, with everything they made
# (history, scores, posts, chat). Hobby Hub chat messages are deleted after CHAT_RETENTION_DAYS.
DATA_RETENTION_DAYS=90
CHAT_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# The biggest song file people can add, in MB (Stage 12). Changed it? Run: docker compose up -d --build
MAX_UPLOAD_MB=100

# ─── Monitoring: Grafana (optional, Stage 8) ─────────────────────────────────────
# 1. Add ",monitoring" to COMPOSE_PROFILES above, and remove the # on these three lines.
# 2. Run:  docker compose up -d
# 3. Open http://localhost:3001  (needs ~1-2 GB of RAM)
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://otel-lgtm:4318
# CADDY_TRACING=tracing_on
# CADDY_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://otel-lgtm:4317

# ─── AI: the Free-time Companion (optional, Stage 9) ─────────────────────────────
# Add ",ai" to COMPOSE_PROFILES above, then run:  docker compose up -d
#
# The "ai" profile downloads two things, ONCE:
#   - Ollama, the program that runs AI models (a Docker image of a few GB: it includes
#     NVIDIA GPU support, even if you don't have a GPU)
#   - the models it runs: the chat model below + the embedding model (~0.6 GB)
# Both stay on your disk; later starts are quick. Without the "ai" profile nothing is downloaded.
#
# Chat model, pick one for your PC (RAM it needs while answering):
#   qwen2.5:0.5b  ~0.4 GB download, ~1 GB RAM  - low-end PCs, simple answers
#   qwen2.5:1.5b  ~1 GB download,   ~2 GB RAM  - default, better answers
#   qwen2.5:3b    ~2 GB download,   ~3.5 GB RAM - strong PCs
AI_CHAT_MODEL=qwen2.5:1.5b

# How long the model stays in RAM after a question. Shorter frees RAM sooner,
# but the next question waits for it to load again.
# OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE=5m

# The embedding model (understands the meaning of questions, English and Vietnamese).
# Don't change it unless you know why: another model may need a database migration.
# AI_EMBED_MODEL=paraphrase-multilingual

# To switch the AI off completely, remove the # on the next line (leave it empty after =).
# OLLAMA_URL=

# ─── Away from home (optional, Stage 20) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Recommended: Tailscale, a private network for your own devices. Nothing to set here: install it on this PC
# and on your phones, then run `tailscale serve --bg 80` on this PC. See docs/stage-20.md.
#
# Or a public web address through Cloudflare, with Cloudflare Access in front (only the people you list get in).
# Add "tunnel" to COMPOSE_PROFILES (e.g. COMPOSE_PROFILES=app,tunnel) and fill in these three.
# Without the last two, the tunnel refuses everyone.
#   TUNNEL_TOKEN           Zero Trust → Networks → Tunnels → your tunnel → the token (secret: never share it)
#   CF_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN  e.g. https://nookfamily.cloudflareaccess.com
#   CF_ACCESS_AUD          Access → Applications → your app → "Application Audience (AUD) Tag"
# TUNNEL_TOKEN=
# CF_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN=
# CF_ACCESS_AUD=

# Optional, not the default: on a cloud server behind nginx with HTTPS and a password (docs/deploy-gcp.md).
# Then set BIND_IP=127.0.0.1, HTTP_PORT=8080, HTTPS_PORT=8443 and remove the # on the next line
# (Windows: separate with ; instead of :). nginx talks to Nook on 127.0.0.1:PROXY_PORT (default 8081).
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:deploy/nginx/compose.proxy.yml
# PROXY_PORT=8081

# ─── Coming from MyWeb (the old name)? ───────────────────────────────────────────
# To keep using your old data, remove the # on these three lines (and keep your old POSTGRES_PASSWORD;
# the old default was "myweb"):
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=myweb
# POSTGRES_USER=myweb
# POSTGRES_DB=myweb
